---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Bug1 = `let height = 40;
let age = 10;

if (height >= 48 || age >= 8) {
  console.log("You may ride.");
} else {
  console.log("Sorry, not this time.");
}`;
const Bug2 = `let isWeekend = false;
let isMember = true;

if (isWeekend && isMember) {
  console.log("Come in.");
} else {
  console.log("Members only on weekdays.");
}`;
const Bug3 = `let isFull = false;

if (!isFull === false) {
  console.log("Warning: tank is not full.");
}`;
const Bug4 = `let length = 15;

if (length >= 8) {
  console.log("Okay");
} else if (length >= 12) {
  console.log("Strong");
} else {
  console.log("Weak");
}`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Debug Challenge: Combining Conditions</h1>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>Objective</h2>

          <p>Four short programs about <code>&amp;&amp;</code>, <code>||</code>, and <code>!</code>. Every one of them looks like it says what the English sentence above it says. Three of them do not.</p>

          <p>
            <b>Three kinds of mistake show up in JavaScript.</b> A{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-red">syntax error</b> stops the program before it
            runs. A <b class="text-sn-orange">reference error</b> runs until it
            hits the bad name, so you get <i>partial output and then a
            crash</i> &mdash; that partial output is how you tell it from a
            syntax error. A <b class="text-sn-yellow">logic error</b> runs
            cleanly to the end and gives the wrong answer, which is the one
            that will cost you marks on an exam.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Three of these 4 are logic errors.</b> If you find
            yourself waiting for an error message on those, that is exactly the
            habit this challenge exists to break.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="bugs">
          <h2>The Four Programs</h2>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 1</h3>

          <p>A rider must be at least 48 inches <b>and</b> at least 8 years old. This lets a 40-inch 10-year-old ride.</p>

          <Code
            code={Bug1}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Bug 1"
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 2</h3>

          <p>The gate should open on a weekend <b>or</b> for a member. This turns away a member on a Tuesday.</p>

          <Code
            code={Bug2}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Bug 2"
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 3</h3>

          <p>This should warn when the tank is <b>not</b> full. It stays silent on a half-full tank.</p>

          <Code
            code={Bug3}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Bug 3"
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <p><b>Also:</b> Write the condition the shortest correct way you can.</p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 4</h3>

          <p>A password is strong if it is at least 12 characters. It is okay if it is at least 8. Otherwise it is weak. A 15-character password reports <code>Okay</code>.</p>

          <Code
            code={Bug4}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Bug 4"
            class="mb-3"
          />
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>For each program, give:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>Which kind of mistake it is &mdash; syntax, reference, or logic</li>
            <li>One sentence on what was wrong</li>
            <li>The corrected line of code</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Work them out by reading first. Running the code tells you{" "}
            <i>that</i> something is wrong; it rarely tells you <i>why</i>, and
            on the exam there is no computer.
          </p>

          <p>Worth <b>3 points</b>, graded on completion.</p>
        </section>

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Commence Debugging
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
